From cd94a58412aa93502d7c76f6222586e08a34981d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justin <j@ukzn>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 18:05:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Read the colour block in the check, and three things it
 exposed

With the colours ingested the checker can compare a standing for every student,
not only for those the registrar wrote a code against. ENG-CIVIL, 2026:1, active
cohort of 321: 168 checkable rows become 299, and doing so turned up three
defects the code-only view had hidden.

1. The colour is read where a code is absent. check_student takes the period's
   colour and the previous period's, and uses each where no code was written --
   for the current standing and for the history the trees read. The prior now
   comes from the period immediately before, not from the newest decision on
   file, which could be years old: Bisseru's 2023 RISK was being read as her
   2026 history while every period since was green.

2. The semester figure ignored supplementary results. The ERS prints "Block 1"
   and "Block 1 Main&Supp" and decides on the second; we used the first, so a
   student who failed in the main block and passed the supp read as short.
   period_load now reads a main period with its own supp blocks, deduped.

3. A light load passed in full read as a clear pass. The rate test cannot see
   it -- 40 credits registered and 40 passed is 100% -- so ERS-ORANGE-LOAD adds
   the load test the carry rule already used. 13 students the registrar had at
   RISK were coming out green on this alone.

Also: the normal-load and 75% columns stop before the minimum-progression one,
because cumulative expectation caps at the degree total while the minimum keeps
climbing. A blank there now carries the last stated line forward instead of
reading as "no line", so a fifth-year student is measured against a full degree
rather than against nothing. This reverses the fail-safe chosen for p75 when
there was no evidence either way; there is evidence now and it runs the other
way -- six fifth-years the registrar has green.

Result: 297 of 299 agree. On the 72 periods where the registrar CHANGED a
student's standing -- the subset that is not just carried forward -- 71 agree.
The two left are the completing DC student and the probation-chain student, both
already known and both wanting a person rather than a rule.
